course: dictionary
lesson: 9056
title: Sleet: rain, snow and ice together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sleet is a weather word. It is a mix of rain and snow, or small icy drops that fall from the sky. «It feels so cosy that they forget they are outside — until a sting of icy sleet reminds them how cold their toes are.» The words icy and sting tell you that sleet is cold and hurts a little.
explain: Sleet can also be a verb. To sleet means that sleet is falling. You can say «It is sleeting» or «It sleets», just as you say «It is raining». Notice the spelling: sleet, sleeted, sleeting.
explain: Context helps again. «Then a small sting of frozen sleet on the skin woke them as from a dream.» Frozen tells you that sleet is a kind of ice, and woke tells you it was sudden and cold. Rain, snow, sleet and hail are all called precipitation, which means water that falls from the clouds.
fact: Sleet is a mix of rain and snow, or small icy drops that fall from the sky.
fact: To sleet means that sleet is falling: «It is sleeting».
fact: Sleet happens when the air is close to freezing.
fact: Sleet makes roads and paths icy and slippery, so take care when you walk.
fact: Rain, snow, sleet and hail are all called precipitation.
fact: Weather forecasts often say «rain turning to sleet».
exercise: What is sleet? | A kind of thick fog that hides the sun | *A mix of rain and snow, or small icy drops | A hot wind that blows across the sand
exercise: «It is sleeting» means what? | Someone is sprinkling sugar on a cake | The sun is shining brightly | *Sleet is falling from the sky
exercise: Rain, snow, sleet and hail are all called what? | *Precipitation | Fog | Thunder
test: Why is sleet dangerous on a path? | *It makes the path icy and slippery | It turns the path into a soft carpet of moss | It makes the path dry and warm
test: In the sentence «a small sting of frozen sleet on the skin woke them», what does frozen tell you? | Sleet is a colour | *Sleet is a kind of ice | Sleet tastes sweet | Sleet is warm and soft
exam: Which sentence uses sleet as a verb? | I like sleet more than sunshine | Sleet and snow fell together | The sleet was cold on my face | *It sleets all afternoon, so bring a coat
